 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Depends on the number of source views $M$, the number of attention heads $H$, and the sequence length (neighbors + views). Attention is $O(M \cdot N_{neigh}^2)$ or similar depending on implementation.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Stores feature volumes for all source views and embeddings for all neighbors and views.
-*   **Performance:** Generalizes to unseen scenes without per-scene finetuning [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Explores deep relationships among multiple views; enforces local 5D radiance field consistency [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Requires a large dataset of diverse scenes for training; pre-trained U-Net dependency [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Time and Memory Complexity:** Not given in the sources.
+*   **Measured Performance:** Not given in the sources.
+*   **Scaling:** Not given in the sources.
+*   **Strengths and Limitations:** TransNeRF explores deep relationships amongst multiple views and aggregates multi-view information into a coordinate-based scene representation via attention, overcoming the limitations of pooling-based fusion models that ignore local consistency of the 5D radiance field [S1, unofficial]. It generalizes to unseen scenes without per-scene finetuning [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When to use it:** Used for novel view synthesis, generalizing to unseen scenes without per-scene finetuning after training on a large dataset of diverse scenes [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-TransNeRF was proposed to address the limitations of pooling-based multi-view representations that ignore local consistency and complex relationships across views [S1, unofficial]. It builds on the Transformer architecture's success in NLP and CV [S1, unofficial]. It is used for novel view synthesis, generalizing to unseen scenes [S1, unofficial].
+TransNeRF was proposed in the paper "Generalizable Neural Radiance Fields for Novel View Synthesis with Transformer" [S1, unofficial]. It builds on the concept of Neural Radiance Fields (NeRF) [1] but adapts it to a generalizable setting using a Transformer framework. It addresses the limitations of previous pooling-based multi-view representations by using attention mechanisms to capture complex relationships across multiple views and local consistency along rays [S1, unofficial].
 
